## 1. Introduction
The simultaneous presence of organic pollutants and heavy metals causes to great concerns for environment and human health [Asuha et al.2010, Diao et al.2016].   Among various techniques for toxic materials removal from water, the adsorption method could be preferred as an efficient method for the removal of heavy metal ions and organic pollutants from water [Da’na.2017, Vunain et al.2016,Boamah et al.2015, Thakur and Voicu.206, Voisin et al.2017,Zhao et al.2018]. The electrospun nanofibers due to the porous structure and high surface area as well as their easier reusability were applied for adsorption of various pollutants from aqueous solutions [Aluko et al.2017, Ma et al.2013, Beheshti et al.2016, Rad et al.2014, Keshtkar et al.2013, Chauque et al.2016, Etemadi et al.2017]. Chitosan based-nanofibers with large specific surface area and microporous structure as well as having functional groups for chelating with various pollutants of wastewater were investigated for effective removal of toxic materials from water [Zhang et a;.2016, Najafabadi et al.2015, Razzaz et al.2016]. However, the used of pure chitosan nanofibers due to its poor stability is limited [Aliabadi et al.2013]. Various polymers have been blended/grafted with chitosan base formulations to improve its performance for wastewater treatment [Shariful et al.2017, Min et al.2015, Habiba et al.2017, Karim et al.2019]. Poly(Nvinylcaprolactam) as a biodegradable/biocompatible temperature sensitive polymer was grafted with chitosan and its application was extensively studied for biomedical applications [Lemus and Claverie.2016, Prabaharan et al.2008, Rejinold et al.2011]. A good balance of intramolecular and intermolecular interactions such as strong hydrogen-bonding interactions in copolymer blends could be resulted in high efficiency of synthesized copolymers [Rana et al. 1993; Rana et al. 1996; Rana et al. 2000]. However, there is a little study about the use of chitosan-grafted-poly(Nvinylcaprolactam) (chitosan-g-PNVCL) copolymer for metal ions removal. Recently, García et al. [Garcia et al.2019] investigated the potential of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL copolymer for separation of metal ions.  However, there is no report about the investigation of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL nanofibers for metal ions adsorption. Furthermore, by incorporating of inorganic materials into the nanofibers, the removal capability of nanofibers toward the wastewater treatment could significantly increase [Aliabadi et al.2014, Koushkbaghi et al.2016, Mohamed et al.2017, Ji et al.2012, Lu et al.2017, Hua et al.2019, Efome et al.2019, Jamshidifard et al.2019]. The various nanomaterials including nanoparticles [Aliabadi et al.2014], graphene oxide [Najafabadi et al.2015, Koushkbaghi et al.2016], carbon nanotubes [Beheshti et al.2016, Mohamed et al.2017], nanozeolites [Rad et al.2014, Ji et al.2012] and metal organic frameworks (MOFs) [Lu et al.2017, Hua et al.2019, Efome et al.2019, Jamshidifard et al.2019] have been embedded into the nanofibers to enhance their adsorption efficiency. MOFs as a new class of crystalline porous materials due to having tunable pore sizes and large porosity as well as the high specific surface area and good compatibility with polymeric matrix could improve their capacity for wastewater treatment [Efome et al.2019, Jamshidifard et al.2019]. However, the use of pure MOF due to its poor stability in water is limited. Therefore, the stability of MOFs could be enhanced by loading of MOFs into the nanofibers. In previous study, the potential of UiO-66 doped- PAN/chitosan nanofibers was studied for the removal of heavy metal ions from aqueous solutions by our groups [Jamshidifard et al.2019]. In another studies, the various MOFs have been incorporated into the PAN nanofibers for the removal of lead, cadmium, mercury, and zinc from aqueous solutions by Efome et al. [Efome et al.2018, Efome et al.2018, Efome et al.2018]. Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks-8 (ZIF-8) as a stable MOF, with large specific area and large molecules have a high potential for trapping of metal ions and organic molecules such as phenol [Zhang et al.2019, Hua et al.2018, Zhou et al.2019]. Furthermore, the high capability of ZIF-8 for adsorption of water pollutants could be achieved via the interaction between hydrogen bonding and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking of ZIF-8 and adsorbates [Zhang et al.2019]. Zhang et al. [Zhang et al.2019] synthesized the magnetic ZIF-8 for U(VI) adsorption which the maximum adsorption capacity was found to be $277.7\ \mathrm{mg.g^{-1}}$ . Huo et al. [Huo et al.2018] fabricated the core-shell ferrite/ZIF-8 nanocomposite adsorbent for the arsenic adsorption from water. Zhou et al. [Zhou et al.2019] indicated that the more than 95 and $80\%$ simultaneous removal of copper and norfloxacin were achieved in the presence of ZIF-8 MOFs adsorbent. Li et al. [Li et al.2019] examined the simultaneous adsorption of U(VI) and humic acid using MWCNT/ZIF-8 composite adsorbent. Liu et al. [Liu et al.2019] used the sulfurized-ZIF-8-filter paper for $\operatorname{Hg}(\operatorname{II})$ sorption which the maximum adsorption capacity was $925.9\mathrm{mg.g^{-1}}$ . Li et al. [Li et al.2018] incorporated the ZIF-8
MOF nanoparticles into the PVDF membrane for continuous removal of $\mathrm{{Ni(II)}}$ from water. Jin et al. [Jin et al.2018] investigated the phenol removal in the presence of PDMS/PVDF/ZIF-8 nanofibrous membrane. However, there was no report for the removal of metal ions and phenol using chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ZIF-8 composite nanofibers. In this work, the potential of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers was investigated for reduction of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol  from aqueous solutions in single and ternary systems. The adsorption kinetic, and adsorption isotherms were used to obtain the information about the adsorption mechanism in the presence of composite nanofibers. The effect of ZIF-8 content, pH, contact time, initial concentration of adsorbates and temperature on the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol was evaluated. The reusability of nanofibers was also examined. In ternary system, the central composite design was applied to investigate the interaction influence of initial concentrations of selected metal ions and phenol on the performance of nanofibrous adsorbent.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Chitosan (low molecular weight),  N-Vinylcaprolactam (NVCL), 2,20-Azoisobutyronitrile (AIBN), 1-ethyl-3-(3-dimethylaminopropyl), carbodiimide (EDC), N-hydroxy succinimide (NHS), 3-mercaptopropionic acid (MPA), Tri sodium polyphosphate (TPP) provided from Sigma-Aldrich (Germany) were used for synthesis of chitosan-g-PNVCL. Zinc(II) nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}.6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and 2-methylimidazole supplied from Sigma-Aldrich (Germany) were applied to synthesize the ZIF-8 MOF nanoparticles. Methanol, acetic acid and other solvents were purchased from Merck (Germany).
## 2.2 Synthesis of chitosan-g-PNVCL copolymer
The chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL copolymer was synthesized via the free radical polymerization as described previously by Prabaharan et al. [Prabaharan et al.2008]. Briefly, the carboxilated PNVCL was synthesized by polymerization of NVCL with MPA as a crosslinker and AIBN as an initiator at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $8\mathrm{{h}}$ . Then, the synthesized product was washed with deionized water and was dialyzed in a cellulose membrane tubing (molecular weight cutoff: 2 kDa, Sigma-Aldrich) for 3 days. Finally, the obtained product was freeze-dried. The carboxilated PNVCL was grafted to the chitosan using EDC and NHS as the condensing agents. First, the chitosan dissolved in $1\%$ acetic acid was added into the carboxilated PNVCL solution at room temperature. Then, the EDC/NHS mixture $(2{:}1~\mathrm{w}/\mathrm{w})$ was drop-wisely added into the solution under stirring for $8\mathrm{{h}}$ at room temperature. Then $0.5\%$ TPP was added into the chitosan-g-PNVCL nanoparticles. Finally, the synthesized product was dialyzed in a cellulose membrane tubing (molecular weight cutoff: $10{-}12\mathrm{kDa}$ , Sigma-Aldrich) for 3 days and was freeze-dried.
## 2.3 Synthesis of ZIF-8 MOF nanoparticles
ZIF-8 MOF nanoparticles were synthesized by dissolving Zn(NO3)2·6H2O) and 2- methylimidazole in methanol under stirring for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ .  The final product was centrifuged and dried in a vacuum oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$
## 2.4 Synthesis of electrospun chitosan-g-PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers
The electrospinning solution was prepared by dissolving $10\mathrm{\wt.\%}$ chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL in $0.5\%$ acetic acid solution under stirring for $4\mathrm{{h}}$ . The prepared solution was placed in a ${5}\mathrm{mL}$ plastic syringe equipped with a syringe needle (19 gauge nozzle). The electrospinnig conditions were high voltage of $20\mathrm{kV}$ , tip-collector distance of $15\mathrm{cm}$ , and flow rate of $0.5~\mathrm{mLh^{-1}}$ . To synthesize the chitosan- $\mathbf{g}.$ -PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers, the various contents of ZIF-8 (1, 2, 3, 5, and $10\mathrm{wt.}\%$ ) were slowly added into the chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL solution and was stirred for $6\mathrm{{h}}$ . At higher concentrations than $10\mathrm{\mt.}\%$ , the electrospinning of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ZIF-8 solution due to the high viscosity of solution and aggregation of ZIF-8 MOFs into the solution before electrospinning process, was difficult. The electrospinning conditions were same for preparation of copolymer nanofiber and ZIF-8 loaded-nanofibers.
## 2.5 Adsorption experiments in a single system
The adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol from aqueous solution using synthesized fibers in a single mode was carried out studied in $250\mathrm{mL}$ Erlenmeyer flasks containing $50\mathrm{mg}$ of the adsorbent in $100~\mathrm{mL}$ of $20\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ adsorbates as a function of ZIF-8 concentration $(0.10\mathrm{wt.}\%)$ , pH (2-7), contact time $(5\mathrm{-}60\mathrm{min})$ ), and initial concentrations of adsorbates $\mathrm{10{-}500\ m g.\ L^{-1}})$ in a batch system. The prepared $1000\mathrm{mg.L^{-1}}$ stock solution of phenol was stored in a brown colored glass bottle to avoid its photooxidation [Chaudhary & Balomajumder 2014]. The adsorption capacity $\left(\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\left(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}\ \mathbf{g}^{-1}\right)\right.$ ) has been investigated as follows:
$$
q_{e}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})V}{1000M}
$$
where $\mathrm{C}_{0}$ and $\mathrm{C_{e}\left(m g L^{-1}\right)}$ are the initial and equilibrium concentrations of adsorbate, respectively. V and M are the aqueous solution volume (mL) and the weight of adsorbent (g), respectively. The kinetic and equilibrium data of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol adsorption were fitted by Pseudofirst-order, Pseudo-second order kinetic and Freundlich, Langmuir and Redlich-Peterson isotherm models. These models were given as follows:
$$
q_{t}=q_{e}(1-\exp(-k_{1}t))
$$
Pseudo-second-order qt  12k q t
Freundlich
$$
q_{e}=k_{\scriptscriptstyle F}C_{e}^{1/n}
$$
Langmuir
$$
q_{e}=q_{m}\frac{b C_{e}}{1+b C_{e}}
$$
Redlich-Peterson $q_{e}=\frac{P C_{e}}{1+\alpha C_{e}^{\beta}}$
