## 2.7 Characterization tests
The X-ray powder diffraction (XRD, Philips PW 1800) was used to determine the structure of the synthesized ZIF-8 MOF using Cu Kα radiation ( $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $30\mathrm{mA}$ ) ranging from $10^{\circ}$ to 80 with a scanning speed of $0.03^{\circ}\left(2\theta\right)\mathbf{s}^{-1}$ . The Fourier transform infrared spectrometer (FTIR, Nicolet Nexus 670, Nicolet Instrument Co., Madison, WI, USA) was used to determine the surface functional groups of the synthesized copolymers. The morphology of ZIF-8 and nanofibers were detected using a scanning electron microscopy (SEM, JEOL JSM-6380) after gold coating.  The final concentrations of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ and As(V) in aqueous solutions were determined using an inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectrophotometer (ICP 6000 Thermal Electron). The final concentration of phenol was also measured using a UV–Vis spectrophotometer (JAS.CO V-530, Japan) at wave lengths of $271\ \mathrm{nm}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1 Characterization of ZIF-8 MOF, copolymer and MOF/copolymer nanofiber
The TEM image of ZIF-8 MOF is illustrated in Fig.1a. As shown, the rhombic dodecahedrons of ZIF-8 were synthesized which in good agreement with crystalline structure of ZIF-8 MOF as previously published [Liu et al.2019]. The surface TEM image indicated that the relatively uniform ZIF-8 MOFs with average particle size of $65\mathrm{nm}$ were fabricated. The XRD pattern of ZIF-8 MOF and MOF-loaded nanofibers are also presented in Fig.1b. The diffraction peaks at $2\uptheta{=}7.1^{\circ}$ , $10.3^{\circ}$ , $12.8^{\circ}$ , $18.2^{\circ}$ , $26.5^{\circ}$ , and $29.8^{\circ}$ corresponding to (001), (002), (112), (222), (334) and (244) diffraction planes of the ZIF-8 indicated the crystalline structure of ZIF-8 MOFs [Huo et al.2018, Zhou et al.2019]. By loading MOF into the copolymer, the intensity of ZIF peeks was decreased which indicated that the crystallinity of MOF through the electrospinning process was decreased. There was no obvious peak for nanofibers which showed the amorphous structure of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL nanofibers.
The functional groups of chitosan, carboxilated PNVCL and PNVCL/chitosan and their interactions were determined using FTIR analysis which results are illustrated in Fig. 1c. For carboxilated PNVCL, the appeared bond in the range $3300{-}3400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was due to the carboxylic groups in the synthesized polymer. The observed stretching vibrations at 2965 and $3092\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the –CH aliphatic groups in the structure of the carboxilated PNVCL. The absorption bands of the amide I and stretching vibration of C-N were detected at $1630\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1480\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , respectively.  Another the characteristic bands of -CH and C-N stretching at $1438~\mathrm{cm^{-}}$ 1 and 842, $720\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were observed in the FTIR spectrum of carboxilated PNVCL.  For chitosan, the observed bands at $3350\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $2780\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1650\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1570\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1410\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1375\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , 1323 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1150\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the hydroxyl, acetylated amino, amide I,  amide II, $\mathbf{-CH}_{2}$ , - $\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ , amide III and C-O-C in the chitosan matrix. In the FTIR spectrum of PNVCL/chitosan, all of the characteristic vibrations of PNVCL and chitosan were detected. The appeared bands at 1621 and $1522\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ could be attributed to the new bands of amide I and amide II formed by the interaction of  amine groups of chitosan and carboxyl groups of carboxilated PNVCL [Prabaharan et al.2008].
Fig.1 (a) TEM image of ZIF-8, (b) XRD patterns of ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 laoded-nanofibers and (c) FTIR spectra of PNVCL, chitosan, and PNVCL/chitosan nanofibers
The SEM images of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ZIF-8 nanofibers with different ZIF-8 content (0-10 wt. $\%$ ) are presented in Fig.2. As shown, the smooth chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL fibers without beads with an average diameter of $235\mathrm{nm}$ were obtained. By loading ZIF-8 up to $3\mathrm{wt.}\%$ , the homogenous fibers with gradual enhancement in fiber diameter due to increase in the solution viscosity were prepared. The average diameter of nanofibers containing 1, 2, and 3wt. $\%$ ZIF-8 were 290, 340 and $390\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The physical immobilizing of ZIF-8 into the nanofibers could be resulted in increasing the fiber diameter. By incorporating 5 and 10 wt. $\%$ ZIF-8 into the chitosan-g-PNVCL fiber, the beaded-fibers duo to higher viscosity of solution and instability of solution jet were produced. Some ZIF MOF particles were aggregated on the nanofibers surface (Fig.2e, f).
## 3.2 Adsorption in a single system
The effect of ZIF-8 concentration in the chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL fibrous matrix on the $\operatorname{As}(\mathrm{V}),\operatorname{Cr}(\mathrm{VI}).$ , and phenol removal under $0.5\mathrm{g/L}$ adsorbent dosage, initial concentration of $20\mathrm{mgL^{-1}}$ , contact time of $60\mathrm{min}$ , pH of 3 and temperature of $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is illustrated in Fig. 3a. The large specific area of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL nanofibers and the electrostatic interaction between functional groups of copolymer such carboxylic, and amide with metal ions and phenol was responsible for high adsorption capacities of As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , and phenol using synthesized chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL
nanofibers [Garcia et al.2019]. By loading ZIF-8 up to $3\%$ , the adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{As(V)}$ , $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , and phenol were increased and then the adsorption capacity of chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ ZIF8 nanofibers was decreased at  higher concentrations than 3 wt. $\%$ ZIF-8. The $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions between the phenol and the imidazole rings of ZIF-8 were increased by loading ZIF-8 into the nanofibers which resulted in increasing the adsorption capacity of phenol [Li et al.2019].  The ion exchange between functional groups of ZIF-8 and metal ions may be responsible for increasing adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{As(V)}$ , and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by increasing ZIF-8 content in the nanofibers matrix up to 3 wt. $\%$ [Huo et al. 2018]. The decrease in adsorption capacities of As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , and phenol in the presence of  chitosan-g-PNVCL/ ZIF-8 nanofibers  containing $5\%$ and $10\%$ ZIF-8 could be attributed to the aggregation of ZIF-8 nanoparticles on the nanofibers matrix as confirmed by SEM analysis. Therefore, chitosan-g-PNVCL/ ZIF-8 3 wt.% nanofibers was selected for further experiments.
The effect of solution $\mathrm{pH}$ on the As(V), $\mathrm{Cr}(\mathrm{VI})$ , and phenol sorption using , chitosan-g-PNVCL/ ZIF-8 3 wt. $\%$ nanofibers under $20\mathrm{mgL}^{-1}$ initial concentration, $0.5\ \mathrm{g/L}$ adsorbent, $60\mathrm{min}$ , and 25 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is presented in Fig. 3b. As can be seen, the optimum pH value for $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol sorption was about 3. There were the more positive ions on the chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ ZIF-8 nanofibers surface for electrostatic attraction with the negatively charged chromate, arsenate and phenol $(\mathsf{p K}\mathord{\sim}10)$ species at pH of 3. The repulsive force between anionic adsorbate and adsorbent led to reduce the adsorption capacities of Cr(VI), As(V) and phenol at pH values above 3. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{\pH_{\mathrm{\pzc}}}$ of synthesized nanofibers was 3.2. The adsorbent surface is charged positively in the solution with $\mathrm{pH}$ below $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{pzc}}$ and negatively above $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{pzc}}$ [Thanh et al. 2018]. At pH values higher than 3.2, the negatively charged surface repulsing the negative species of Cr(VI), As(V) and phenol via electrostatic repulsion resulted in reduction of removal efficiency of negatively charged-adsorbates. The competition of hydroxyl groups in the water and adsorbates for chelating with functional groups of nanofibers led to decrease the adsorption capacities at pH higher than 3. Thus, the pH of 3 was selected for further experiments. The adsorption experiments were carried out at various times to achieve the equilibrium time for effluents sorption using chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ ZIF-8 nanofiber under optimum pH of 3, $0.5\ \mathrm{g/L}$ adsorbent dosage, $20\mathrm{mgL^{-1}}$ initial concentration , and $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ which results are presented in Fig.3c. As can be seen, the adsorption capacities of As(V), and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ by the fibers were sharply increased during the first $20\mathrm{min}$ and reached the equilibrium after $30\mathrm{min}$ . Whereas the phenol sorption reached the equilibrium at $40\mathrm{min}$ . The fast sorption of metal ions compared with phenol molecules indicated the greater affinity of nanofibrous adsorbent for metal ions sorption than for phenol molecules. Also, the hydrophobic nature of phenol molecules and their weaker interactions with active sites of nanofibrous adsorbent resulted in the slower adsorption of phenol molecules using nanofibers. The kinetic parameters are presented in Table 2a. As can be seen, the kinetic data of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol sorption using nanofibers was best fitted by the pseudo-second order kinetic model (figure is not shown).
The results of As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ and phenol sorption by the chitosan- $\mathbf{g}$ -PNVCL/ ZIF-8 nanofibers under various concentrations of effluents are illustrated in Fig.3d. The adsorption capacity was increased by an enhancement in the initial concentration via an enhancement in driving force and then, the saturation of active sites of adsorbent led to sorption efficiency did not significantly change by further increase in the initial concentration due to the saturation of active sites of adsorbent. The isotherm parameters are presented in Table 2b (figure is not shown.). The best fitting of experimental data with Redlich-Peterson isotherm models indicated that there were both homogenous and heterogeneous sorption cites of synthesized nanofibers for metal ions and phenol sorption. The maximum experimental adsorption capacities of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol were 495.6, 439.5 and $395.8\ \mathrm{mg.g^{-1}}$ , respectively. The weak Vander Waals interaction of hydrophobic phenol molecules and function groups of adsorbent could be responsible for lower adsorption capacity of phenol using nanofibers compared with the strong electrostatic interaction of metal ions with functional groups of nanofibrous adsorbent.  The capability of nanofibers for As(V), Cr(VI) and phenol  sorption were compared with other nanofibrous adsorbent in the literature (Table 3) [Bhaumik et al.2012; Gundogdu et al.2012; Avila et al.2014, Chauhan et al.2014; Bhaumik and Noubactep.2015; Karthik and Meenakshi.2015; Li et al.2015; Meenakshi.2015; Min et al.2015; Najafabadi et al.2015; Tao et al.2015; Beheshti et al.2016; [Mohamed et al.2017 ]. As shown, the sorption capacity of synthesized nanofibers toward Cr(VI), As(V) and phenol removal was higher than that of other nanofibrous adsorbents in the literature.
The possible mechanisms of As(V), and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ removal using chitosan-g-PNVCL nanofibers were (i) the surface adsorption of metal ions, (ii) across of metal ions from inner pores of nanofibers and their trapping in the copolymer matrix, and (iii) ion exchange between the functional groups of copolymer such as amide, and carboxylic with positively charged-metal ions.  Whereas, the surface adsorption, Vander Waals interaction between phenol molecules and adsorbent and π-π stacking could be responsible for adsorption of phenol from aqueous solution. By loading ZIF-8 into the nanofibers, the physical adsorption and π-π interactions were increased which resulted in increasing the adsorption capacity of ZIF-8 loaded-fibers toward phenol, As(V), and $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ removal from water.
The synthesized nanofibers were regenerated in $0.2\mathrm{M}\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ and reused for five sorption/desorption cycles which results of As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ and phenol adsorption are presented in Fig. 3e. As can be seen, the adsorption capacities of As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ and phenol were slightly decreased from 36.8,  35.8 and 32.6 mg.g-1 to 32.1, 30.6 and $28.5\mathrm{mg.g^{-1}}$ after five
sorption/desorption cycles. This behavior may be attributed to the release of MOF nanoparticles for surface/pores of nanofibers by time. Furthermore, destroying the frame work of some MOF during regeneration of composite nanofibers with ${\bf H N O}_{3}$ , may be responsible for decreasing the adsorption capacity after five sorption/desorption cycles. The obtained results revealed the excellent potential of nanofibers in practical applications.
