## 3.3 Simultaneous removal of As(V), $\mathbf{Cr(VI)}$ , and phenol
Analysis of variance (ANOVA) results for the removal efficiency of As(V), Cr(VI), and phenol in the range $10{-}50\ \mathrm{mg/L}$ showed that the linear and some square and interaction parameters were significant (Table 4). The full quadratic models were given as follows:
$Y_{1}=104.934-0.2666x_{1}-0.2736x_{2}-0.1056x_{3}+0.0017x_{2}^{2}+0.00147x_{1}x_{2}+0.00172x_{1}x_{2}+0.001472x_{2}-0.0015x_{1}+0.0015x_{2}.$ 3   (Eq. 7) $Y_{2}=103.51-0.3171x_{1}-0.2329x_{2}-0.1309x_{3}+0.00197x_{2}^{2}-0.00091x_{1}x_{2}+0.00109x_{1}x_{3}-0.00078x_{2}-0.00091x_{1}x_{2}.$ 3  (Eq. 8) $Y_{3}=10.34-0.3378x_{1}-0.4398x_{2}-0.5756x_{3}+0.0028x_{2}^{2}+0.0036x_{3}^{2}+0.0009484x_{1}x_{2}+0.00197x_{1}x_{3}+0.0017$ 8x  3 (Eq. 9) where, $\mathbf{Y}_{1}{-}\mathbf{Y}_{3}(\%)$ are the As(V), $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , and phenol removal efficiency, respectively. The high values of $\mathbb{R}^{2}\left(\mathbb{R}^{2}>0.98\right)$ ) indicated a high reliability of the predicted models. The maximum removal efficiencies of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ , As(V) and phenol in optimum conditions under initial concentrations of $10\mathrm{mg/L}\mathrm{As(V),10\mathrm{mg/L}C r(V I)}$ and $10\mathrm{mg/L}$ phenol were predicted to $98.98\%$ , $96.85\%$ and $87.9\%$ were in good agreement with the experimental data.
The simultaneous effect of the two parameters is investigated using surface plots of the process parameters which results are presented in Fig. 4. As can be seen, by increasing the initial concentration, the effluents, the removal efficiencies were gradually decreased. The reduction of porosity and blockages of nanofibers pores resulted in decreasing removal efficiency by increasing initial concentrations of adsorbates. Also, the faster saturation of pores at higher concentrations of adsorbates resulted in decreasing the adsorption capacities of Cr(VI), As(V) and phenol.  Furthermore, the inhibitory effect of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions concentration on the removal percentages of As(V) and phenol was more than that of inhibitory effects of  As(V) and phenol concentrations on the $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ removal. This indicated the more favorability of nanofibrous membrane for the removal of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ compared with As (V) and phenol reduction. Therefore, the adsorption cites of nanofibers were most saturated with $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions. The various parameters such as ionic radius and electronegativity may be resulted in the higher adsorption of $\mathrm{Cr(VI)}$ ions compared with As (V) and phenol.
